# Chroma content pack, Politics, Elections and Public Office (modern Earth first, with the tribal and magic worlds).
# PART 1 of the pack's moments: DOORS AND HOOKS (moments 1 to 6 of MOMENTS-BRIEF.md): two everyday pairs whose options
# take a first title by the act (campaign volunteer, party member, polling-station volunteer, council candidate, local
# party officer, campaign organiser, constituency caseworker, political adviser, pollster), and two echoes that open the
# pathway from a life's own history (a local fight, a debate at school).
# Syntax: header of chroma-library/build.py. Rules: chroma-library/library-spec.md and politics/MOMENTS-BRIEF.md.
# Names in title:, grants:, requires:, holds: and aims: come from politics/catalogue.py, core/reach.py and the base
# catalogue. World lines follow politics/worlds.py. Everyday pairs split the calls 2 and 3; echoes are even on their own
# (five one-color options and one cross cycle).

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- pair A, open to anyone (no holds): calls WG (cycle s1) / calls UBR (cycle s1)

== leaflets to deliver before Saturday
tier: everyday
rate: 0.006
times: open to anyone: in a year with an election about 8 in 100 teenagers and adults are asked to help a local campaign, fewer in other years; about 3 to 4 US adults in 100 do campaign work in an election year (ANES), and about 8 lives in 100 volunteer at some point (catalogue share; estimate) (packs, 2026-10-07, Emren's ask for parliament in every game: no birth draw, so the door can come to every life that wants it, at a rate that keeps about today's numbers)
gap: 3-8
stages: juvenile+
age: 14-90
calls: WG
tone: joy
life: community, public life
horizon: week
roles: friend, elder, neighbour, the candidate's volunteer
earth: a candidate's team leaves a box of leaflets and a map of streets at the door
tribal: a speaker's kin ask for someone to walk to the far hearths and speak for him before the gathering
magic: a faction's herald needs broadsheets carried through the lower wards before the lots are cast
scene: A volunteer from a local candidate's team is at the door with a box of four hundred leaflets and a photocopied map, three streets marked in yellow. They are short of hands, and every leaflet has to be through a letterbox before Saturday.
scene W: Every household ought to hear from every candidate before it votes; that is how an election is meant to work. The three streets in yellow are the ones nobody has covered.
scene U: {N} reads the leaflet on the doorstep: three promises, a bar chart with no scale, and a photograph of the candidate outside the library that is about to close.
scene B: The candidate's face is on half the lampposts this month. Whoever wins will remember who walked the streets for them, and who did not.
scene R: Last week the candidate stopped at the bus stop and talked to {N} like a person rather than a vote. An evening out on the streets, with a reason to be there, sounds good.
scene G: The streets in yellow are the ones {N} has walked since childhood, and half the names on the doorbells are names {N} knows.
scene tribal: The speaker's sister comes to {Ns} hearth at dusk. The gathering is a moon away, the far hearths have not heard her brother's case, and someone with good legs and a straight tongue must walk there and speak for him.
scene magic: A herald in faction colours sets a bundle of broadsheets on {Ns} step, the ink still wet. The lots are cast in five days, and nobody has carried them through the lower wards.
- W | take the map, and get a leaflet through every door on it by Friday night | v: conformity, universalism | title: campaign volunteer | self_control: + | tribal: walk to every far hearth before the gathering, and speak for him at each one | magic: carry a broadsheet to every door in the lower wards before the lots | chance: 75
- U | read every candidate's leaflet first, then join the party whose sums add up | identity | v: self-direction, universalism | title: party member | tribal: hear every speaker's kin out, then bind yourself to the faction that makes most sense | magic: read every faction's broadsheet, then swear to the one whose sums add up | chance: 75
- B | deliver your share, and ask the candidate for ten minutes over coffee afterwards | v: achievement, power | tribal: speak at the far hearths, then ask the speaker for a place by his fire | magic: carry your share, then ask the candidate for a word at the tavern after | chance: 55
- R | grab the box and do the whole estate tonight, with music in your ears | v: stimulation, hedonism | title: campaign volunteer | body: light | tribal: set off for the far hearths tonight, singing the speaker's name along the path | magic: run the broadsheets round the lower wards tonight, whistling as you go | chance: 70
- G | deliver your own street only, where people know your face and will stop to ask | v: benevolence, tradition | title: campaign volunteer | tribal: speak for him only at the hearths of your own kin | magic: carry the broadsheets down your own lane only, where every door knows your face | chance: 80
- W>U | say you will help once you have heard every candidate at the public meeting | door | v: universalism, self-direction | tribal: say you will speak for nobody until every speaker has been heard at the fire | magic: say you will carry nothing until every faction has spoken in the square | chance: 70
- U>B | skip the streets that never vote, and bin their leaflets behind the shops | v: achievement | mark: hid a wrong | closed: approval: binning leaflets the team asked to be delivered; backfire: a neighbour finds them in the bin and tells the candidate | self_control: - | tribal: skip the hearths that never stand behind anyone, and say you went | magic: skip the lanes that never cast a lot, and drop their broadsheets in the canal | chance: 75
- B>R | say no: Saturday is for your own plans, and nobody is paying for this | v: hedonism, self-direction | tribal: say no: you have your own hunting to do before the gathering | magic: say no: the herald can pay a runner | chance: 95
- R>G | talk a friend into coming, and make an evening of it with chips after | door | v: benevolence, hedonism | aims: campaign volunteer | tribal: talk a friend into walking along, and make a night of it at the far fire | magic: talk a friend into coming, and finish the round at the tavern | chance: 75
- G>W | deliver them with the old neighbour who has walked every election for forty years | v: tradition, conformity | mark: learned a skill | tribal: walk the hearths with the old woman who has carried words for every speaker since her youth | magic: walk the lanes with the old herald who has carried every faction's broadsheets for forty years | chance: 75
works: By Friday night every door on the map has had its leaflet, and the team thanks {N} by name.
works: Two neighbours stop {N} in the street to ask about the candidate, and {N} finds there is plenty to say.
fails: Rain soaks half the box on the first evening, and the rest goes round late and creased.
fails: A dog, a broken gate and a man who shouts about all politicians: by the second street {N} has had enough.

# Round 5, 2026-10-07 (P14): value tags read from the act on 1 option (check.py section 4).
== poll workers wanted for election day
tier: everyday
rate: 0.006
times: open to anyone: about 3 adults in 100 a year see a call for polling-station staff they could answer; the US staffed about 774,000 poll workers in 2020 (Pew Research Center 2024), and about 3 lives in 100 work a polling station at some point (catalogue share; estimate) (packs, 2026-10-07, Emren's ask for parliament in every game: no birth draw, so the door can come to every life that wants it, at a rate that keeps about today's numbers)
gap: 3-8
stages: juvenile+
age: 14-90
calls: UBR
tone: mixed
life: community, work
horizon: week
roles: neighbour, friend, the station's supervisor
earth: the council needs clerks for the polling stations, a fee and a fifteen-hour day
tribal: the elders need someone with a clear head to keep the counting stones at the gathering
magic: the Order asks for witnesses at the warded urns, under its seal, for a day's silver
scene: A notice from the town hall goes up at the library and online: poll clerks wanted for election day. Training on Tuesday evening, a fee for the day, and the doors open at seven in the morning and close at ten at night.
scene W: Someone has to hand out the ballot papers, check the names and seal the boxes, or none of it counts. The notice says the stations are short of staff again this year.
scene U: The training covers the register, spoilt papers, and what to do when a name is missing. {N} would like to see how the whole machine fits together.
scene B: The fee is decent for one day of sitting at a table, and it is paid whoever wins. A day inside the station also shows exactly how power gets counted.
scene R: Fifteen hours in a school hall, then the count: boxes tipped onto tables, candidates pacing, the result read out after midnight. {N} wants to be in the room for that.
scene G: The polling station is the old village hall, where {Ns} grandparents voted and where the same faces have sat behind the trestle table for as long as anyone remembers.
scene tribal: The elders want a steady hand to keep the counting stones when the band casts its pebbles at the gathering: one bowl for each speaker, and no stone dropped or added.
scene magic: A clerk of the Order nails a notice to the guild hall door: witnesses wanted at the warded urns from dawn to the evening bell, under the Order's seal, for a day's silver.
- W | sign up, go to Tuesday's training, and sign the declaration of secrecy | binds | v: conformity, universalism | title: polling-station volunteer | tribal: take the elders' charge, and swear by the fire to keep the stones true | magic: swear the Order's oath of the urns, and take its seal for the day | chance: 75
- U | ask for the clerks' handbook, and learn the rule for every odd case | v: self-direction, achievement | mark: learned a skill | title: polling-station volunteer | self_control: + | tribal: ask the elders how every hard case at the last gathering was settled | magic: borrow the Order's book of the urns, and learn every rule in it | chance: 60
- B | take it for the fee and the day off, and say so plainly | v: security, achievement | title: polling-station volunteer | tribal: keep the stones for the share of meat the elders promise | magic: take the seal for the day's silver, and say so plainly | chance: 75
- R | put your name down for the count, the loud late part after the doors close | v: stimulation | title: polling-station volunteer | tribal: ask to count the pebbles at the end, when every eye is on the bowls | magic: ask to stand by the urns when they are broken open at night | chance: 65
- G | pass this year, and leave it to the people who have done it for years | v: tradition | tribal: pass, and leave the stones to the old hands who always keep them | magic: pass, and leave the urns to the witnesses who stand there every time | chance: 92
- W>U | ask the council how the count is checked, and only then put your name down | v: security, self-direction | aims: polling-station volunteer | tribal: ask the elders how the stones are checked before you agree | magic: ask the Order's clerk how the urns are warded before you agree | chance: 80
- U>B | work out the fee by the hour, and decide your day is worth more than that | v: achievement, security | tribal: weigh the meat against a day's hunting, and decide to hunt | magic: reckon the silver against a day at your craft, and keep to your craft | chance: 90
- B>R | sign up for the fee, then cry off on the morning for a better offer | v: hedonism | mark: broke your word | closed: approval: dropping out on the day; backfire: the station opens short-handed, and the name comes off the council's list | self_control: - | tribal: agree to keep the stones, then slip off to a feast at the next camp | magic: take the seal, then send word of a fever and go to the fair | chance: 75
- R>G | offer to drive the old neighbours to the polls instead | v: benevolence | mark: helped someone in need | tribal: offer to walk the old ones to the gathering, carrying what they cannot | magic: offer your cart to take the old folk of the lane to the urns | chance: 85
- G>W | work the station in the village hall where your family has always voted | v: tradition, conformity | title: polling-station volunteer | tribal: keep the stones at the fire where your mother kept them in her day | magic: stand witness at the urns in your own ward's hall, as your family always has | chance: 55
works: The day runs long but smoothly, and the boxes leave under seal on time.
works: The station's supervisor thanks {N} at the end of the night, and asks {N} back for the next election.
fails: A queue out of the door at seven, a register with a page missing, and {N} goes home wrung out.
fails: The council's list is already full, and {N} is not called this time.

