== a paid job on the campaign
tier: everyday
rate: 0.03
times: per campaign volunteer, local party officer or party member of a year or more, a year, about 3 in 100 young adults, 2 in 100 adults and 1 in 100 in midlife hear of paid campaign or office work they could take, most of it in big election years; about 1 life in 500 works a paid campaign and 1 in 330 as an adviser (catalogue shares; estimate) (packs, 2026-10-07: no birth draw; party members too, so each color has a way into party work)
gap: 3-8
stages: young_adult adult mature elder
age: 18-85
calls: WUG
holds: campaign volunteer | local party officer | party member
tenure: 1-100
tone: joy
life: work, public life
horizon: months
roles: friend, boss, colleague, partner
earth: a campaign needs paid staff for six weeks, and someone has passed your name on
tribal: a speaker who wants to be chief needs someone to walk the valley for him all summer, fed at his fire
magic: a faction's house hires clerks and runners for the season of the lots, with board and a wage
scene: {friend} has passed {Ns} name to a campaign that is hiring: six weeks of paid work before polling day, perhaps more after if the candidate wins. It wants an organiser, a caseworker for the office, a researcher who can write, and someone for the data desk.
scene W: The campaign has a code of conduct, a spending limit and an agent who signs off every leaflet. {N} likes that the job comes with rules.
scene U: The job list reads like a map of how campaigns really work: target lists, canvass returns, polling, casework. {N} wants to see the inside of the machine.
scene B: The pay is modest, but the people who work a winning campaign tend to get the jobs that follow it.
scene R: Six weeks of doorsteps, late nights and a result at the end of it. {N} can already feel the noise of the count.
scene G: The campaign office is above the old bakery on the high street, and the work would be on streets {N} has known for years, for people {N} has known as long.
scene tribal: A speaker who means to be chief of the clans needs someone to walk the valley for him all summer, from band to band, carrying his words and bringing back what is said. Whoever goes will eat at his fire.
scene magic: A faction's house posts a notice in its window: clerks, runners and a reckoner wanted for the season of the lots, with board, a wage, and a letter of character at the end.
- W | apply for the agent's post at the party's area office, with references from the branch | v: conformity, security | title: party official | grants_if_fails: a long shot that missed | tribal: offer to keep the speaker's count of gifts and debts between the bands, with a word from the faction's elders | magic: apply for the clerk's post at the faction's house, with a letter from your ward's warden | chance: 4
- U | send the data desk the model you built of the last election | v: achievement, self-direction | title: pollster | requires: data analyst | without: impossible | tribal: tell the speaker which bands leaned his way last summer, hearth by hearth, and offer to keep that count | magic: send the house your tally of the last lots, guild by guild | chance: 12
- B | take the adviser's job, and set your price before the first day | v: power, achievement | title: political adviser | requires: graduate | without: impossible | tribal: offer to sit behind the speaker at the fire, for a share of every gift he gets | magic: offer to serve as the candidate's secretary, and name your wage first | chance: 12
- R | say yes to whatever they have, and be on the doorsteps by Monday | v: stimulation | title: campaign organiser | tribal: say yes, and be on the valley path by first light | magic: say yes, and be running the wards by Monday | chance: 7
- G | take the caseworker's job on your own high street, and carry your neighbours' troubles | v: benevolence, universalism | title: constituency caseworker | tribal: offer to carry the troubles of your own hearths to the speaker, and see them settled | magic: take the petitions post in your own ward, and carry your neighbours' petitions to the Crown | chance: 12
- W>B | ask for the contract, the hours and the pay in writing, then answer | v: security, conformity | aims: campaign organiser | tribal: ask the speaker, before witnesses, what you will be fed and for how long | magic: ask for the terms under the house's seal before you answer | chance: 75
- U>R | turn it down, but ask to sit in on the data desk on polling night | door | v: self-direction, stimulation | aims: pollster | tribal: turn it down, but ask to sit with the band's ear when the pebbles are counted | magic: turn it down, but ask to watch the house's reckoner on the night the urns open | chance: 85
- B>G | turn it down, and put your cousin forward, who needs the work more | v: benevolence, tradition | tribal: send your sister's son in your place, since he needs a big man's favour more | magic: put your cousin forward for the runners' post instead | chance: 65
- R>W | take the caseworker's job, because the people who ring that office have nobody else | v: benevolence, universalism | title: constituency caseworker | tribal: offer to carry the families' troubles to the speaker, because nobody else will | magic: take the clerk of petitions' post, because the petitioners have nobody else | chance: 12
- G>U | keep the day job: the family needs your wage and your evenings more than this | v: tradition, benevolence | mark: turned down a chance | tribal: stay with the band: the hunt needs your spear more than the speaker needs your legs | magic: stay at your craft: the household needs the steady wage and you at the table | chance: 90
works: {N} starts on Monday, and by the second week the work has a rhythm and a purpose.
works: The campaign asks {N} to stay on after polling day, whatever the result.
fails: The post goes to someone with more experience, and {N} hears nothing more.
fails: Two weeks in, the money dries up, and the campaign lets half its staff go.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- echoes: a local fight (cycle s3), a debate at school (cycle s4)

# Round 5, 2026-10-07 (P14): value tags read from the act on 3 options (check.py section 4).
== the fight that made you want to stand
tier: echo
rate: 0.005
once: yes
stages: young_adult+
age: 18-95
times: per person who met a local fight, a protest, an injustice or a bitter election in the last three years: about 1 in 10 feel the pull to stand in the years after; many councillors say a local fight first brought them in (estimate)
tone: mixed
life: community, public life
horizon: months
roles: friend, neighbour, rival, colleague
earth: the fight you lost or won still makes you angry, and the next election is close
tribal: the quarrel at the fire is over, but you keep thinking you could have spoken for the band yourself
magic: the guild's ruling still stings, and the town council will sit again in spring
after: moment 'your town faces a change you could fight', 'a protest to save the local hospital', 'a sense of injustice', 'a bitter election' or 'they ask you to lead because you stood up once' within the last three years, and no elected office held (no [local councillor], [mayor] or [member of parliament])
delay: 0-3
likelier: an election within the year; the fight was settled by a council vote; friends or family in a party, a union or a cause; time to spare
rarer: stress above 1.5; heavy care duties; a move away from the place of the fight; a settlement everyone could live with
scene: Months after the fight, {N} still finds the arguments running on the way to work. Now the posters are going up for the next council election, and the same names are on them.
scene W: The decision was made by people who never read the objections, under rules nobody local had a say in. {N} keeps thinking that rules are only as good as the people at the table.
scene U: {N} has worked out exactly where the fight was decided: one committee, three votes, a report nobody challenged. Next time, someone who has read the papers should be in that room.
scene B: The people who decided were not cleverer or better; they simply had the seats. {N} has started to wonder why {N} has never had one.
scene R: It still makes {Ns} blood boil. Every time {N} passes the place, the old anger comes back, and with it a voice that says: then do something.
scene G: The fight was about a place {N} loves and the people who live there. Whether caring for it needs a seat on the council is the question {N} keeps turning over.
scene tribal: The quarrel at the council fire ended a moon ago, and the band went the way the loudest voices wanted. {N} keeps thinking of the words that could have been said, and of who should have said them.
scene magic: The guild's ruling went against the ward, sealed and nailed to the hall door. The town council sits again in spring, and its seats are open to anyone with a faction's seal or enough names.
- W | stand for the council in May, to change the rules from the inside | identity | v: universalism, conformity | title: council candidate | tribal: ask to be heard at the council fire this winter, to change the band's custom from within | magic: stand for the town council in spring, to change the rules from inside the hall | chance: 70
- U | read how the decision was made, and join the party whose plans would have stopped it | v: self-direction, universalism | title: party member | tribal: hear how the elders decided, and bind yourself to the faction that argued your side | magic: study the guild's ruling, and swear to the faction that argued against it | chance: 92
